# config/Kconfig.timing — Scheduler, WCET, networking timers, watchdog
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  All timing/WCET constants with per-arch and per-profile
# defaults matching the current codebase values.

menu "Scheduling, IPC & timing"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduler
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SCHED_HZ
    int "Scheduler tick frequency (Hz)"
    range 10 1000
    default 100  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 100  if PROFILE_EDGE && BOARD_QEMU
    default 250  if PROFILE_EDGE && BOARD_VF2
    default 250  if PROFILE_EDGE && BOARD_K1
    default 500  if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 100
    help
      Periodic timer interrupt rate.  Drives RR scheduler preemption,
      WDT kick, and tickless-idle `set_next_tick_smart`.
      Higher SCHED_HZ reduces task-switch latency but increases ISR
      overhead.  QEMU TCG amplifies ISR cost ~5× — keep it lower there.
      Current codebase: 100 Hz (crates/drivers/irqchip/src/clint.rs AtomicU32).

config SCHED_HZ_FLOOR
    int
    default 10
    help
      Minimum scheduler tick rate CONFIG.INI is allowed to request at boot
      (Q4.2, owner decision 2026-09-25: CONFIG.INI on the removable FAT
      volume may only LOWER `SCHED_HZ` below the compile-time default it
      was built with, never raise it, and never below this floor). Must be
      kept equal to SCHED_HZ's own `range 10 1000` lower bound — below it
      the WDT kick and RR preemption granularity are unreliable, so a
      CONFIG.INI value under this is treated as garbage and the
      compile-time SCHED_HZ default is used instead (see
      `resolve_sched_hz` in `crates/core/config/src/lib.rs`). Kconfig cannot
      reference another symbol's `range` directly, so this is a second
      literal that has to be kept in sync by hand;
      `crates/core/limits/build.rs` validates `SCHED_HZ_FLOOR <= SCHED_HZ` at
      every build so a drift is a hard build error, not a silent one.

config SCHED_TIMER_HEAP_PER_CPU
    bool "One timer-sleeper heap per CPU (sched-timer-heap)"
    default y
    help
      With the `sched-timer-heap` cargo feature, each CPU keeps its own
      heap of timer sleepers, like Linux's per-CPU hrtimer bases. A task
      that sleeps on a timer is armed on the heap of the CPU it blocks
      on; that CPU's tick ISR pops it and that CPU's tickless comparator
      is programmed from it. Every heap lock is taken only by its own CPU,
      with interrupts masked, so it is never contended across CPUs.

      Off: one heap and one lock for the whole machine (the old shape).
      Every CPU's tick ISR and every timer sleep then spins on the same
      lock with interrupts masked; when the holder's hart is not running
      (QEMU -icount runs harts one at a time; a preempted vCPU), the
      spinning ISR waits until the next virtual deadline (measured: 50 ms
      timer ISRs on riscv64 -smp 2).

      Cost: 12 B x MAX_TASKS per CPU the board has (in each CPU's
      per-CPU area, from the frame allocator at boot; NR_CPUS does not
      scale it), plus 1 B per task slot: 768 B per CPU at the edge
      profile's 64 tasks, 48 KiB per CPU at fleet's 4096. Off, one heap
      (CPU 0's area). Instructions per sleep and per tick: one byte store
      and the hart id read, none of them on another CPU's lines.

config SCHED_TIMER_WAKE_BATCH
    int "Timer sleepers popped per heap lock hold (sched-timer-heap)"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      With the `sched-timer-heap` cargo feature (a kernel default), a
      tick's pass over due timer sleepers (`timer_heap::wake_due`) pops at
      most this many entries per hold of the sleeper-heap lock, then drops
      the lock to wake them (wakes take the run-queue locks, which never
      nest inside the heap lock). Cost: 16 B of tick-ISR stack per unit
      (the popped and retry arrays), either ISA, and one lock round trip
      (interrupts masked) per BATCH due sleepers. A larger batch holds the
      lock, with interrupts masked on this hart, for one more heap pop
      (O(log MAX_TASKS)) per unit. Raise it only when many sleepers share
      a deadline (periodic tasks on one grid) and the round trips show in
      the tick; lower it when the masked window bounds an interrupt
      latency budget.
      Ignored without the heap (the per-tick sweep has no batch).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# QoS timer tolerance (timer coalescing by QoS tier, after Apple's timer
# leeway). The hint is SYS_SCHEDCTL's form 4 (`libsys::qos_hint`); the reader
# is the timer-sleeper heap (`timer_sleepers::arm`).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE
    bool "QoS timer tolerance: a task's QoS tier lets its timers fire late and share wake-ups"
    depends on SCHED_TIMER_HEAP
    default y if PRESET_DESKTOP || PRESET_IOT_HMI || PRESET_WEARABLE || \
                 PRESET_ROBOT || PRESET_INDUSTRIAL || PRESET_AUTOMOTIVE
    default n
    help
      A timer a task arms (a sleep, a timed wait) may fire up to the slack
      of the task's QoS tier AFTER its deadline, never before. The heap
      arms such a timer at a wake-up that is already scheduled inside that
      window, if there is one, so two sleepers share one interrupt, one
      switch out of idle and one stretch of a deep idle state instead of
      two. The idle governor reads the same heap
      (`nearest_timer_deadline`), so it sees the coalesced schedule and
      picks its state from it.

      Only the fair class has slack. A stop, deadline or real-time task
      gets none, whatever its hint and whatever the tiers below say: its
      timers are its contract (`sc::timer_slack_us` tests the class before
      anything else; ktest `qos_timer_slack_never_real_time`).

      A task that has said nothing (QOS_DEFAULT) or asked for latency has
      the tier values below, 0 unless you raise them: only a task that
      declared itself throughput or background work moves. The wake is
      late by at most the tier's slack; a timed wait that ends by timeout
      ends that much later.

      Cost: one atomic load of the task's SC and one compare per timer arm
      (const-folded away when off), a pass over the armed entries of the
      CPU's heap when the task's slack is non-zero (O(entries), each tested
      against the window first), and 4 B per task slot (the slack the slot's
      entry carries). Off: every timer fires at its deadline.

      Default on for the desktop, IoT/HMI and wearable presets (every
      eligible task) and, with the option below, for the robot, industrial
      and automotive presets (only the rows that opt in).

config QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE_ROWS_ONLY
    bool "Only tasks whose topology row says timer_slack = true get slack"
    depends on QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE
    default y if PRESET_ROBOT || PRESET_INDUSTRIAL || PRESET_AUTOMOTIVE
    default n
    help
      A topology row (CAPS.TOML, format 5) opts its task in with
      `timer_slack = true`; the task's QoS hint then picks the tier. Every
      other task, kernel tasks included, keeps exact deadlines. This is the
      shape for a product whose timing is audited per task (a robot, an
      industrial controller): the signed topology says who may be late.

      Off (with QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE on): every fair task has its tier's
      slack, the row's key is read and ignored.

config QOS_SLACK_DEFAULT_US
    int "Timer slack of QOS_DEFAULT, the hint every task starts with (us)"
    range 0 1000000
    default 0
    help
      How late a timer of a task that never set a QoS hint may fire (see
      QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE). 0: exact, which is what every kernel loop and
      every task of an existing image keeps. Raise it to coalesce
      everything that has not asked for latency; a value near the scheduler
      tick (1 / SCHED_HZ) is where coalescing starts to pay. Ignored
      without QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE.
      Surfaces as `azos_limits::QOS_SLACK_DEFAULT_US`.

config QOS_SLACK_LATENCY_US
    int "Timer slack of QOS_LATENCY (us)"
    range 0 1000000
    default 0
    help
      The tier of a task that said latency matters more than throughput
      (interactive, a control loop that is not real-time). 0 keeps its
      deadlines exact; it is a knob, not a recommendation to raise it.
      Ignored without QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE.
      Surfaces as `azos_limits::QOS_SLACK_LATENCY_US`.

config QOS_SLACK_THROUGHPUT_US
    int "Timer slack of QOS_THROUGHPUT (us)"
    range 0 1000000
    default 2000
    help
      The tier of a batch job: its timers may be late by this much to
      share a wake-up with another sleeper. Ignored without
      QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE.
      Surfaces as `azos_limits::QOS_SLACK_THROUGHPUT_US`.

config QOS_SLACK_BACKGROUND_US
    int "Timer slack of QOS_BACKGROUND (us)"
    range 0 1000000
    default 20000
    help
      The tier of housekeeping (a sync, a policy re-check, a poll nobody
      waits for): its timers may be late by this much, so they ride on
      whatever else wakes the CPU instead of waking it. Ignored without
      QOS_TIMER_TOLERANCE.
      Surfaces as `azos_limits::QOS_SLACK_BACKGROUND_US`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wave-4 scheduler placement and sleeper options (U02-3). Each is a kernel
# cargo feature that forwards to azos_sched; all three are on in every
# configuration, the way the kernel's old `default` list had them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config SCHED_O1_PLACEMENT
    # cargo-feature: sched-o1-placement base
    bool "O(priorities) wake placement (a per-priority load histogram)"
    default y
    help
      Wake placement (`find_best_cpu`) queries a histogram of runnable tasks
      per priority and hart, kept by `hist_reaccount` after every write to a
      field the score depends on, instead of scanning MAX_TASKS. Off: the
      scan, and no histogram to maintain. The maintenance is paid on every
      block and wake, the saving on machines with more than one hart online.

config SCHED_TIMER_HEAP
    # cargo-feature: sched-timer-heap base
    bool "Timer sleepers in an indexed min-heap"
    default y
    help
      Timer sleepers live in a min-heap: `wake_expired_timers` and
      `nearest_timer_deadline` visit only the due sleepers instead of
      sweeping MAX_TASKS every tick. Off: the per-tick sweep.

config SCHED_IPC_AFFINITY
    # cargo-feature: sched-ipc-affinity base
    bool "Fast-IPC hand-off wakes co-locate on the waker's hart"
    default y
    help
      A fast-IPC call or reply wakes its peer on the waker's hart when
      nothing there outranks it (`ipc_wake_target_cpu`), and the fast path
      is enabled (`IPC_FAST`). Off: the plain wake target.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dispatch classes and the scheduling context (N6, owner answers Q6-Q8)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config SCHED_CLASS_STOP
    def_bool y
    help
      The highest class: the per-CPU stop slot, and the exempt safety loops
      (actuation, e-stop, watchdog), which run above DL, RT and fair work.
      Not optional: a build-time assert in crates/core/sched/src/classes.rs
      refuses a kernel without it.

config SCHED_CLASS_DL
    bool "Dispatch class: deadline (EDF + CBS reservations)"
    default y
    help
      Tasks holding an admitted EDF + CBS reservation (topology `deadline`
      rows, the reservation syscall). Off: admission refuses every
      reservation with EOPNOTSUPP (reason `class-off`), no hart ever has
      reservation state, and the reservation paths are never entered (they
      fold to the band-only code). Costs nothing per switch when no
      reservation is admitted. Turn off only for a build with no deadline
      task at all.

config SCHED_CLASS_RT
    bool "Dispatch class: real-time band (fixed priority)"
    default y
    help
      The fixed-priority band: tasks below RT_PRIORITY_THRESHOLD, not
      time-sliced, with the per-hart band budget (RT_BAND_CAP_PCT of each
      RT_BAND_WINDOW_MS). Off: no task is in the band; band-level priorities
      are scheduled as fair levels and the band budget is never charged.
      Every robot profile needs it (the motor, IMU and watchdog loops).

config IRQ_THREADED
    bool "Interrupts as scheduled objects: the handler runs on a hard reservation"
    depends on SCHED_CLASS_DL
    default y if RT_HARD || RT_MIXED
    help
      An interrupt line is bound to an event port by an op of its Device
      capability (DEVICE_IRQ_BIND), and its handler is the task that binds
      it. With this on, that task must hold a hard reservation (EDF + CBS):
      the bind is refused (-EPERM) without one, because its time would not
      be budgeted, and the line is routed to the CPU the reservation was
      admitted on. A handler that runs out of budget has its lines masked
      until the reservation is replenished, and the overrun leaves a
      decision record (irq-budget). So an interrupt storm costs at most one
      top half per handler run and cannot take time from the tasks admitted
      beside it: the line's cost is inside the CPU's booked load.

      The kernel's own lines (the console UART, the virtio devices) keep a
      minimal top half (mask, complete, notify) and run their bottom halves
      in one kernel thread on the boot CPU, on a reservation of its own
      (IRQ_THREAD_PRIORITY, IRQ_THREAD_BUDGET_PCT, IRQ_THREAD_PERIOD_US). The
      timer keeps its top half and hands the lease expiry sweep to that
      thread. The thread's lines are riscv64's and aarch64's: x86_64's IOAPIC
      loses an edge that arrives while an entry is masked, so its kernel
      lines stay in the interrupt handler.

      A message-signalled interrupt (an `msi.<k>` slot of a topology row) is
      delivered to ring 3 where the platform has it (x86_64, riscv64 with
      AIA). It cannot be masked, so while its handler is out of budget its
      deliveries are dropped instead of held.

      Off: the bind takes any task, nothing is budgeted, the kernel's lines
      run whole in the interrupt handler, and none of this code runs.
      Default on for the hard and mixed real-time classes.

config IRQ_THREAD_PRIORITY
    int "IRQ thread: priority"
    range 1 11
    default 9
    help
      The priority (real-time band, 0 most urgent) the per-boot-CPU kernel
      thread that runs the bottom halves of the kernel's own lines is
      admitted at, with IRQ_THREADED. Below the actuation loops (8), above
      the watchdog (11) by default. The thread is a hard reservation, so it
      runs in the deadline class, ahead of the band; this number orders it
      against the other reservations of its CPU. A ring-3 handler runs at the
      priority its own reservation was admitted at, and a task that waits on
      a handler across IPC lends it its priority through the usual donation.
      Only read with IRQ_THREADED.

config IRQ_THREAD_BUDGET_PCT
    int "IRQ thread: budget (% of a CPU)"
    range 1 50
    default 10
    help
      The share of its CPU the kernel IRQ thread may use, with IRQ_THREADED:
      its hard reservation's runtime over IRQ_THREAD_PERIOD_US. When it runs
      out, the kernel lines it serves stay masked until the next period, so a
      device that storms the console UART or a virtio queue cannot take
      more than this share (admission books it like any reservation). The
      console's own output is driven through the UART line, so a share that
      is too small slows it down. Only read with IRQ_THREADED.

config IRQ_THREAD_PERIOD_US
    int "IRQ thread: period (us)"
    range 200 100000
    default 1000
    help
      The replenishment period of the kernel IRQ thread's reservation, with
      IRQ_THREADED. A line held masked by an overrun waits at most this long.
      Only read with IRQ_THREADED.

config SCHED_CLASS_FAIR
    def_bool y
    help
      The fair class: every task outside the band and idle, scheduled
      EEVDF (earliest eligible virtual deadline first, crates/core/sched/
      src/eevdf_core.rs): each task has a weight from its priority (Linux's
      nice table, DEFAULT_PRIORITY = nice 0, 1.25 x per level) and the CPU
      is shared by weight; the pick is the eligible task with the earliest
      virtual deadline, so a short slice is a low wake-up latency, not a
      bigger share. Not optional: a build-time assert in
      crates/core/sched/src/classes.rs refuses a kernel without it. There is
      no round-robin alternative: no preset needs one (a certifiable
      profile gets its bounded latency from the RT band and the DL class).

config FAIR_SLICE_US
    int "Fair class: slice (us)"
    range 100 100000
    default 3000
    help
      The run a fair task asks for before its virtual deadline (Linux's
      base_slice is 0.75 ms x (1 + log2 ncpus)). Longer: fewer switches among
      CPU-bound tasks; shorter: a woken task is served sooner. The timer tick
      (1 / SCHED_HZ) is the preemption point, so a slice below it only moves
      deadlines. Per task it will come from the QoS class (the `qos_id` hook
      in `fairq::params`, not wired).
      Surfaces as `azos_limits::FAIR_SLICE_US`.

config SCHED_CLASS_IDLE
    def_bool y
    help
      The idle class: the per-CPU idle task. Not optional (every CPU needs
      something to run); asserted at build time like SCHED_CLASS_FAIR.

choice
    prompt "Default dispatch class of a new task (SCHED_DEFAULT)"
    default SCHED_DEFAULT_LEGACY
    help
      The class a task created without a class request starts in. Not DL:
      a deadline task needs an admitted reservation. Costs nothing at
      run time (written once at task creation).

    config SCHED_DEFAULT_LEGACY
        bool "By priority (band -> RT, idle -> idle, else fair)"
        help
          Today's behaviour: the creation priority decides the class.

    config SCHED_DEFAULT_FAIR
        bool "Fair, unless the priority is the band or idle"
        help
          Same as by-priority except that the SC records fair explicitly.

    config SCHED_DEFAULT_RT
        bool "RT for every non-idle task (static RT appliances only)"
        depends on SCHED_CLASS_RT
        help
          Every task starts in the RT class. For a static profile whose
          every task is a periodic control loop; the band budget still
          applies.

endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Energy-aware scheduling (RFC-0051, stages E0-E4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config ENERGY
    # cargo-feature: energy
    bool "Energy-aware scheduling: utilisation tracking and the energy model"
    default n
    help
      RFC-0051 stages E0-E4. Emits the kernel cargo feature `energy`
      (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py).

      The seams themselves (Placement, Governor, IdleGovernor in
      crates/core/energy) are in every build with today's behaviour only:
      Legacy wake-up placement, no frequency request (the boot clock), `wfi`
      in both idle tasks. With this option off they fold to the code they
      replaced: the kernel's .text is unchanged (invariant I4).

      y = also track per-task and per-CPU utilisation (ENERGY_UTIL, updated at
        every dispatch, switch-out, wake-up and tick), and read the energy
        model at boot: the signed topology's `[energy.domain.*]` sections,
        else the DTB's operating-points-v2 / idle-states, else none. The
        model is validated and reported (`[ENERGY]` lines); an invalid one is
        refused and the machine runs with none. The E3 frequency governor
        (Schedutil, DeadlineFloor) and the E4 idle governor run only when the
        topology carries a model and mode `balanced` or `endurance`; they
        count their decisions and apply none (no clock driver; every idle
        state is entered as `wfi`). User space can change the governor, the
        OPP range of each domain, the headroom, the rate limit and an
        idle-depth limit at run time (the `power` endpoint;
        `POWER.ELF serve` applies a profile); the kernel refuses a value
        below the real-time deadline floor or the WCET reference OPP and
        records the refusal. Cost, measured
        under QEMU -icount by the gate's energy row (one run per ISA and
        tracker, 2026-10-03): switch hook 73-76 instructions per call on
        riscv64 and 128-271 on aarch64; tick hook 61-71 and 187-382.
      n (default) = none of the above is compiled.

      The energy MODE (performance / balanced / endurance) is not a Kconfig
      symbol: the signed topology chooses it (`[energy] mode`, invariant
      I5), and every built-in profile topology says `performance`.

choice
    prompt "Utilisation tracker (with ENERGY)"
    default ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW
    help
      How ENERGY ages the utilisation signals. Both are integer and do O(1)
      work per update whatever the gap since the last one. Surfaces as the
      bool consts azos_limits::ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW / ENERGY_UTIL_PELT.
      No effect without ENERGY.

    config ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW
        bool "Window (WALT-like): max(last window, mean of the last four)"
        help
          Busy time per ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW_US window; the signal is the
          larger of the last closed window and the mean of the last four.
          A step to fully busy shows after one window; a task that stops
          reads zero four windows later.

    config ENERGY_UTIL_PELT
        bool "PELT-like geometric decay, half-life 32 periods"
        help
          avg = avg*y + input*(1-y) per ENERGY_PELT_PERIOD_US period, with
          y^32 = 1/2, in Q32 fixed point.

endchoice

config ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW_US
    int "Utilisation window (us, window tracker)"
    range 1000 100000
    default 10000
    help
      Length of one window of the window tracker. No effect without ENERGY
      or with ENERGY_UTIL_PELT.

config ENERGY_PELT_PERIOD_US
    int "Utilisation period (us, PELT-like tracker)"
    range 256 10000
    default 1024
    help
      Length of one decay step of the PELT-like tracker; the half-life is 32
      of them (32.8 ms at the default). No effect without ENERGY or with
      ENERGY_UTIL_WINDOW.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IPC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config LEASE_PRIORITY_INHERITANCE
    bool "Leases: donate a blocked lessor's priority to its lessee (anti-inversion)"
    default y
    help
      RFC-0031. On by default since owner decision round 17 (wave 8).
      Surfaces as `azos_limits::LEASE_PRIORITY_INHERITANCE`; the unused
      branch is const-eliminated.

      y (default) = a lessor blocked in `lease_wait_return` donates its live
        PRIORITY to the lessee holding its buffer, until the lease is
        returned or expires (`azos_sched::donate_priority`, the same
        mechanism the user-driver proxy uses while it waits on a ring-3
        driver; rule in crates/core/sched/src/donation.rs). Priority only, not
        the scheduler class: under the Legacy policy dispatch is by
        priority alone.
      n = no inheritance: the lessor blocks until the lessee is eventually
        scheduled or the lease expires; with a non-expiring lease
        (expire_ticks=0) a runnable mid-priority task delays it without
        bound.

      Reached today only by kernel callers of `lease_wait_return`: no
      syscall arm waits on a lease. Proven by the `sched: lease inversion`
      gate row (`i3-smoke`): with inheritance the lessor's wait ends before
      any mid-priority spinner finishes; without it, after all of them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Control loop
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config CONTROL_TXN_TICKS
    bool "Control plane: transactional ticks (rollback recoverable faults)"
    default y
    help
      Experiment I-13 (RFC-0029) — ACCEPTED, default ON. A per-hart checkpoint
      is armed once at the motor control task's entry; a recoverable fault
      (illegal instruction / misaligned load-store) inside the control loop is
      rolled back — motors safe-stop and the task restarts at its entry —
      instead of `panic=abort` halting the kernel mid-motion.

      y (default) = a recoverable fault in the control loop is survivable
        (safe-stop + restart; abort counted in TXN_ABORTS). Verified:
        survived=1, aborts=1, 0 FATAL (RFC-0029 §Results).
      n = the fault is FATAL (legacy: kernel logs + shuts down). The rollback
        branch is const-eliminated → trap handler byte-identical to legacy.

      Surfaces as `azos_limits::CONTROL_TXN_TICKS`. No-fault hot path is
      unchanged (the arm runs once per task entry, not per tick; the trap-hook
      check only runs on the rare exception path). Recoverable-cause whitelist
      is conservative (excludes page faults + ecall). Remaining hardening:
      cert review of the trap-handler hook (RFC-0017).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real-time band budget (wave 11 SCHED-RT, RFC-0052 §4.3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config RT_BAND_WINDOW_MS
    int "RT band budget: window (ms)"
    range 10 10000
    default 1000
    help
      Length of the per-hart window in which the real-time band (priorities
      below 12, never preempted by the tick) may use at most
      RT_BAND_CAP_PCT of the hart while a non-band task is ready on it. The
      rest of the window is guaranteed to the non-band tasks of that hart
      (net-poll, ring-3 services, best effort). Work-conserving: if nothing
      outside the band is ready, the band keeps the hart.

      A shorter window bounds how long a runaway band task holds the hart
      in one stretch (cap x window) at the price of more throttling events.
      Accounting is by the monotonic counter at the switch and tick points;
      enforcement is a one-shot comparator write, armed only while a band
      task runs AND a non-band task is ready on its hart.

      Surfaces as `azos_limits::RT_BAND_WINDOW_MS`. Cost: see
      RT_BAND_CAP_PCT.

config RT_BAND_CAP_PCT
    int "RT band budget: share of each window the band may use (%)"
    range 50 100
    default 95
    help
      Share of each RT_BAND_WINDOW_MS window the real-time band may run on a
      hart while a non-band task is ready there. 95 (default) guarantees 5 %
      to the non-band tasks, the share Linux's RT throttle and its 6.12
      fair_server default to. 100 disables the budget: a band task that
      never blocks owns its hart (the behaviour before wave 11).

      Admission uses the same cap: the EDF+CBS reservations of band tasks
      admitted on one hart may sum to at most this share of it.

      Cost, measured (wave 11 SCHED-RT rows, -smp 1 -icount, instructions):
      one comparator write through the arming path 39 (riscv64, Sstc) /
      30 (aarch64, CNTV_CVAL); a call that finds the comparator already
      earlier, 11 / 12. Over the rows' 12 s run (rt-motor at 1 kHz on the
      same hart, a runaway band task, EDF and CBS load) the RT paths armed
      ~40,000 times and wrote the comparator ~850 times. With no band task
      involved and no reservation on the hart: one per-hart word test per
      switch, pick and tick; vsbench (bench-minimal, -icount) moved
      sched-yield +4 instructions on both ISAs, switch-loaded +18/+19,
      ipc-roundtrip +20 (aarch64) / -2 (riscv64), syscall-floor 0 / -2.
      Surfaces as `azos_limits::RT_BAND_CAP_PCT`.

# The safety comms timeouts (now in seconds) and PID_DT_MS are in the Robot
# menu (config/Kconfig.robot, "Safety layer and motor control"), wave 11.
config SCHED_SWITCH_COUNTERS
    bool "Count each task's context switches (voluntary / preempted)"
    default n if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default y
    help
      Per-task counts of the switches a task took voluntarily (yield,
      block, exit) and by preemption, as DOMAIN_TASKINFO, the task's vDSO page
      and Linux getrusage report them. vsbench's switch lanes read them to
      prove that each yield switched.

      Cost: one atomic add on every context switch (about five
      instructions) and 16 bytes per task slot. Nothing safety-related
      reads them (the watchdog and RT budgets keep their own accounting),
      so the embedded product profile leaves them out; off, the counts
      read 0. Turn on wherever switch statistics or vsbench's switch
      lanes are wanted.

config SCHED_REMOTE_WAKE_DEFER
    bool "Defer a contended wake onto another CPU to that CPU"
    default y
    help
      A wake whose task goes to ANOTHER CPU's ready queue tries that
      queue's lock once. If it is free the task is queued at once, as
      before; if it is held (or earlier wakes for that CPU are still
      pending) the task is pushed onto the target CPU's lock-free deferred
      wake list and the reschedule IPI is sent. The target queues it with
      its own lock when it takes that IPI. A wake for the waker's own CPU
      is unchanged.

      Why: a remote wake from any context, the timer ISR included,
      must not spin with interrupts masked on a lock another hart holds;
      the holder may not run until this hart stops (QEMU -icount runs
      harts one at a time; a preempted vCPU does the same).

      Cost: 4 bytes per task slot and per CPU. An uncontended remote wake
      runs the same lock instruction it always did; a contended one costs
      a CAS push, an IPI and a drain on the target instead of the spin.
      Turn off only to measure the old spinning remote enqueue.

config SCHED_SAVING_GATE_US
    int "Longest wait for a picked task's context save (us)"
    range 10 100000
    default 1000
    help
      A hart that picks a task still being switched out on another hart
      waits for that save to finish (the context-saving gate in
      do_schedule) at most this long, then puts the task back on the queue
      instead of hanging. A legitimate wait is a handful of instructions;
      the bound only matters when something is broken.

      Cost: none on a switch whose task is already saved (the common
      case); the clock is read only while waiting. Keep it well below one
      scheduler tick (1/SCHED_HZ) so a wedge cannot hide for a tick, and
      well above any real save time on the slowest board.

config SCHED_SAVING_GATE_CLOCK_EVERY
    int "Spins between clock reads in the context-saving gate"
    range 1 65536
    default 256
    help
      How many spins of the context-saving gate's wait pass between two
      reads of the clock that bounds it (SCHED_SAVING_GATE_US). A clock
      read is a device access on some boards, so it is kept out of the
      tight loop. A power of two compiles to a mask. Only the waiting
      path pays it; lower it if the gate's bound must be precise.

endmenu # Scheduling, IPC & timing
